-- title:
--   Power-of-coordinate membership is invariant under formal coordinate change
-- statement:
--   Let $B$ be a commutative ring and let $\varphi, u, v$ be elements of `Series B`, i.e. pairs $(\varphi_0,\varphi_1)$, $(u_0,u_1)$, $(v_0,v_1)$ of power series in two variables $X_0, X_1$ over $B$ (formally, functions $\mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to B[[X_0,X_1]]$). Assume that each $u_i$ and each $v_i$ has zero constant coefficient, and that $u \circ v$ is the identity pair, where composition is substitution, $(\psi \circ \chi)_i = \psi_i(\chi_0,\chi_1)$; thus $u_i(v_0,v_1) = X_i$ for $i = 0,1$. Assume further that there is an $N \in \mathbb{N}$ such that for each $i$ the power $X_i^N$ lies in the ideal of $B[[X_0,X_1]]$ generated by the two series $\varphi_i(u_0,u_1)$, i.e. by the range of $\varphi \circ u$. The conclusion is that there is an $N \in \mathbb{N}$ such that for each $i$ the power $X_i^N$ lies in the ideal generated by $\varphi_0$ and $\varphi_1$. No hypothesis is imposed on the constant coefficients of $\varphi$, and only the one-sided identity $u \circ v = \mathrm{id}$ is assumed.
--
--   The statement expresses that the condition 'some power of each coordinate lies in the ideal generated by $\varphi$' — the finite-order infinitesimality of the zero locus of $\varphi$ — may be tested after an arbitrary change of formal coordinates, the new coordinates being given by a pair $u$ with a one-sided inverse $v$. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld formal module material, in [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.exists_X_pow_mem_span_act_of_hasStructureConstants_edgeConstants_zero`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.exists_X_pow_mem_span_act_of_hasStructureConstants_edgeConstants_zero), where the hypothesis is available in curvilinear rather than in standard coordinates.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CerednikDrinfeld_FormalODModule_exists_X_pow_mem_span_of_X_pow_mem_span_comp_of_comp_eq_id.lean

